00:02:28.561 --> 00:02:31.228
Use the chat to connect
with other attendees.

00:02:36.133 --> 00:02:37.531
<v Priscilla Afia Opoku> Thank you for the introduction.

00:02:37.555 --> 00:02:40.398
It's an honor to be here
to share my presentation

00:02:40.878 --> 00:02:43.293
and also to share my experience.

00:02:43.317 --> 00:02:47.008
So like mentioned before or earlier,
I am Priscilla,

00:02:47.032 --> 00:02:51.581
and I'll be speaking with you on the topic
Nobody Budgets for Accessibility

00:02:52.030 --> 00:02:53.668
Until You Make the Case.

00:02:54.497 --> 00:02:58.385
This presentation is going to be
a blend of my personal experience

00:02:58.385 --> 00:03:03.836
and then sources from research I did
while preparing for this presentation.

00:03:04.763 --> 00:03:06.963
Let's just get straight into it.

00:03:07.758 --> 00:03:09.984
A little bit of a backstory.

00:03:10.159 --> 00:03:12.371
I am Ghanaian, living in Ghana.

00:03:12.543 --> 00:03:16.134
And in Ghana,
when you have completed the university,

00:03:16.158 --> 00:03:20.027
it is required of you to serve
the nation for a year,

00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:23.040
and we like to call
that the National Service Scheme,

00:03:23.577 --> 00:03:24.912
or NSS for short.

00:03:25.669 --> 00:03:26.881
God being so good,

00:03:27.847 --> 00:03:34.353
last year November, I was recruited
as an NSS personnel at Ecobank.

00:03:35.173 --> 00:03:39.017
Ecobank is a leading
pan-African bank here in Africa.

00:03:42.552 --> 00:03:46.463
I joined the UX team,
that's the user experience team,

00:03:46.487 --> 00:03:49.850
and at the start of this
year, I was tasked by my boss

00:03:49.874 --> 00:03:54.043
to conduct an accessibility
audit on Ecobank's mobile app.

00:03:54.202 --> 00:03:55.667
And then not just that.

00:03:56.016 --> 00:04:00.984
After I've done with the audit, I am
supposed to present it as a business case

00:04:01.008 --> 00:04:02.462
to relevant stakeholders.

00:04:03.115 --> 00:04:08.000
And mind you, I had heard about
accessibility as a UX/UI designer,

00:04:08.424 --> 00:04:14.042
but then I didn't really truly
understand what it meant and entailed.

00:04:14.420 --> 00:04:19.353
So I was nervous about it
because it's a big project.

00:04:20.714 --> 00:04:24.015
What am I going to tell
these relevant stakeholders?

00:04:25.603 --> 00:04:28.156
With someone who doesn't know
much about accessibility.

00:04:30.953 --> 00:04:33.723
There were so many reasons why I could
have backed out of the project.

00:04:34.538 --> 00:04:36.788
First being that it was a big project,

00:04:36.812 --> 00:04:39.247
and then the next reason was that

00:04:39.271 --> 00:04:43.932
accessibility is not really
practiced here in Africa.

00:04:44.206 --> 00:04:47.469
Unlike in European countries
and then in America,

00:04:47.830 --> 00:04:50.883
there are even laws that back
digital accessibility.

00:04:50.885 --> 00:04:55.140
But here in Africa,
and to be specific, in my country,

00:04:55.769 --> 00:05:00.517
though we have laws backing
persons with disabilities,

00:05:00.541 --> 00:05:06.876
but then we do not have laws backing
digital accessibility for such people.

00:05:09.909 --> 00:05:15.351
Down my rabbit, research hole,
I decided to find out.

00:05:15.375 --> 00:05:16.957
This is why I decided to find out

00:05:16.981 --> 00:05:22.580
if Africa, they have laws
backing digital accessibility.

00:05:24.207 --> 00:05:28.965
And I came across that,
okay, not really.

00:05:29.688 --> 00:05:34.793
So I was like, "Fine, I'm just gonna
go right ahead with this audit

00:05:34.817 --> 00:05:36.542
and then just get it over with,"

00:05:36.566 --> 00:05:39.790
because I have been assigned
by it by my boss.

00:05:40.704 --> 00:05:44.544
So I did what anyone else
would have done in my shoes.

00:05:44.544 --> 00:05:49.061
I started off by researching
what accessibility truly meant,

00:05:49.527 --> 00:05:56.609
and then also I needed to understand
why Ecobank needed to be part of this,

00:05:56.633 --> 00:05:59.904
why Ecobank needed to conduct
an accessibility audit,

00:05:59.928 --> 00:06:01.915
and then what was in it for Ecobank?

00:06:04.506 --> 00:06:07.126
So, fast forward in the research phase,

00:06:07.150 --> 00:06:09.179
this is where I discovered that

00:06:09.203 --> 00:06:13.199
there are 1.3 billion people
with disabilities globally,

00:06:13.542 --> 00:06:16.169
and this is from World
Health Organization.

00:06:16.630 --> 00:06:21.384
And this number of people make
up 16% of the world's population.

00:06:21.829 --> 00:06:25.941
I also found out about
the European Accessibility Act,

00:06:25.965 --> 00:06:27.765
that's EAA for short,

00:06:28.310 --> 00:06:32.561
and the American Disability Act,
that's ADA for short.

00:06:33.785 --> 00:06:38.978
I was very amazed to see
these laws being backed—

00:06:39.002 --> 00:06:42.300
backing persons with disabilities
outside Africa

00:06:42.324 --> 00:06:44.294
and just like I said earlier,

00:06:44.318 --> 00:06:47.407
and there's little to none
like this in Africa.

00:06:47.676 --> 00:06:50.367
And as a UX/UI designer,

00:06:51.994 --> 00:06:56.559
with my experience
with designing with my fellow designers

00:06:56.857 --> 00:06:58.536
and then even developers,

00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:03.247
we never really take
digital accessibility into consideration.

00:07:03.254 --> 00:07:08.009
We just go ahead, design,
and then hand over to the developers

00:07:08.033 --> 00:07:11.687
and then they go ahead, bring
it live, and then it's out there.

00:07:12.330 --> 00:07:18.888
You barely hear of designers here in
Africa talk about digital accessibility.

00:07:20.336 --> 00:07:23.165
So in the same research phase,

00:07:23.189 --> 00:07:31.413
I moved on to find out if Africa was having
laws backing persons with disabilities

00:07:31.437 --> 00:07:35.005
and if we really did
practice digital accessibility.

00:07:35.323 --> 00:07:39.662
And then I found out that
according to the UN statistics,

00:07:39.979 --> 00:07:43.841
there are 80 million persons
with disability living in Africa,

00:07:45.445 --> 00:07:47.513
and this is where Ecobank thrives.

00:07:47.884 --> 00:07:55.282
There are 34 Ecobank branches,
if I should say, in Ecobank.

00:07:57.391 --> 00:08:02.607
So there are 34… Ecobank is represented
in 34 different countries.

00:08:04.625 --> 00:08:08.138
And then these 80 million
people with disabilities,

00:08:08.264 --> 00:08:12.224
that's a whole set of customer
range we are ignoring.

00:08:13.014 --> 00:08:15.857
So I was beginning to understand that,

00:08:15.881 --> 00:08:20.394
okay, then the need for this audit
is really, really necessary

00:08:20.723 --> 00:08:26.630
because we may be ignoring
these set of customers,

00:08:26.985 --> 00:08:28.832
which are worth 30 million people.

00:08:32.859 --> 00:08:35.374
Still in the rabbit, research rabbit hole,

00:08:35.398 --> 00:08:39.425
I got to understand the various
benefits a company enjoys

00:08:39.714 --> 00:08:42.413
when they adopt the principle
of accessible design.

00:08:42.892 --> 00:08:46.845
So I got to find out that some benefits
are that there is more traffic

00:08:46.869 --> 00:08:48.288
on your digital product.

00:08:49.307 --> 00:08:53.100
More traffic here,
I mean that you have more and more people.

00:08:53.102 --> 00:08:55.096
So imagine 80 million people,

00:08:55.644 --> 00:08:59.276
80 million new people getting
on board on your digital app

00:09:00.034 --> 00:09:02.444
or getting on board
on Ecobank's mobile app.

00:09:02.884 --> 00:09:04.299
That's like more traffic.

00:09:04.543 --> 00:09:05.893
And then the more the traffic,

00:09:05.917 --> 00:09:09.166
the more the chance the company
stands in winning more money

00:09:09.190 --> 00:09:10.586
or getting more money.

00:09:11.237 --> 00:09:17.726
So another benefit
is also reduced lawsuits

00:09:17.750 --> 00:09:22.963
for countries that have legal laws
backing digital accessibility.

00:09:23.401 --> 00:09:30.201
And then also there's the need for
better search engine optimization.

00:09:31.502 --> 00:09:33.837
And then also not forgetting
your brand perception.

00:09:34.515 --> 00:09:39.206
If people are able to
identify your company as,

00:09:39.230 --> 00:09:41.784
oh, they are practicing
inclusiveness.

00:09:44.352 --> 00:09:47.189
It gives better perception
to your customers,

00:09:47.213 --> 00:09:51.408
not just your customers,
like others other than your customers,

00:09:51.946 --> 00:09:53.070
about your company.

00:09:53.468 --> 00:09:54.802
So most--

00:09:55.619 --> 00:10:02.169
I also realized that most companies fail to
adopt this principle of accessible design,

00:10:02.642 --> 00:10:05.810
and I think this would be because

00:10:05.834 --> 00:10:11.641
they don't really and truly
understand yet the benefits

00:10:11.665 --> 00:10:16.170
in terms of profit, in terms of revenue,
that accessibility could bring

00:10:16.194 --> 00:10:20.546
or give to their respective companies.

00:10:21.265 --> 00:10:24.347
So at this point,
I was very much convinced that, okay,

00:10:24.371 --> 00:10:26.532
I am ready to conduct the audits.

00:10:26.554 --> 00:10:30.257
I am ready to go down the hill
and conduct the audits.

00:10:30.630 --> 00:10:34.270
Now, in order to start
the audit, I needed to plan.

00:10:35.429 --> 00:10:37.430
I needed to plan the audit,

00:10:37.454 --> 00:10:40.947
and before I started
with the planning itself,

00:10:41.271 --> 00:10:43.463
I had to prepare documents

00:10:43.487 --> 00:10:48.358
to the stakeholders I was going to
make a business case for—sorry, to.

00:10:48.932 --> 00:10:53.281
So these stakeholders
included the group head,

00:10:53.711 --> 00:10:56.166
who was the chief digital officer,

00:10:56.497 --> 00:10:59.461
and then also the head of digital
business channel management.

00:10:59.825 --> 00:11:07.284
So this meeting was to just prepare them
for what is ahead.

00:11:08.992 --> 00:11:12.553
I believe I did this because I
needed them to be a part of the journey

00:11:13.562 --> 00:11:14.852
right from the start,

00:11:14.876 --> 00:11:16.908
so it doesn't become stressful

00:11:17.354 --> 00:11:22.130
and more difficult to get them at the end
of the phase or the whole process,

00:11:22.530 --> 00:11:26.370
just to get them to hear what I
have to say about accessibility.

00:11:30.999 --> 00:11:33.657
I made a… there were several
meetings that were held

00:11:33.681 --> 00:11:39.721
where I presented my findings on what I
had learned about accessibility as a whole,

00:11:40.155 --> 00:11:42.135
what accessibility meant,

00:11:42.135 --> 00:11:48.402
the benefits of digital accessibility,
the legal laws,

00:11:48.426 --> 00:11:51.237
countries that practice
digital accessibility

00:11:51.261 --> 00:11:55.010
and the laws that were backing
this digital accessibility.

00:11:55.791 --> 00:11:58.050
Sorry, this is digital accessibility.

00:11:59.567 --> 00:12:07.523
So they were—I guess they were
much more enthusiasts about it,

00:12:07.570 --> 00:12:09.214
like the way I was.

00:12:10.005 --> 00:12:19.204
It got them to really understand
what digital accessibility truly means.

00:12:19.263 --> 00:12:24.091
It got them to prepare their minds
for the audit that I was about to make.

00:12:24.826 --> 00:12:26.985
And then they gave me the go-ahead

00:12:27.009 --> 00:12:34.336
to go ahead, conduct the audit, and then we
discussed the next steps after the audit.

00:12:35.469 --> 00:12:38.625
Okay, so this is where I actually
start the planning of the audit.

00:12:39.685 --> 00:12:48.250
In the planning stage, I decided to go
with the WCAG 2.2 level AA as a criteria,

00:12:48.539 --> 00:12:51.283
while I adopted the POUR principles,

00:12:51.307 --> 00:12:58.307
which is perceivable, operable, understandable,
and robust principles, as the framework.

00:12:59.940 --> 00:13:05.072
I discovered this criteria
and then this POUR principle

00:13:05.096 --> 00:13:07.394
from the research I did earlier

00:13:08.294 --> 00:13:10.473
when preparing to meet
with the stakeholders

00:13:10.497 --> 00:13:12.640
just to prepare their minds
for what was ahead.

00:13:15.923 --> 00:13:20.992
I plan to use the Accessibility
Scanner as an automated tool.

00:13:23.014 --> 00:13:27.516
Accessibility Scanner is a free Android
app by Google that helps developers

00:13:29.726 --> 00:13:33.740
and designers identify
accessibility issues in their apps,

00:13:33.764 --> 00:13:36.837
and it also provides
actionable recommendations.

00:13:37.954 --> 00:13:42.337
In the auditing phase,

00:13:43.091 --> 00:13:44.779
there were some issues that were detected.

00:13:45.405 --> 00:13:49.915
Some issues were some text were too
faint against background to read.

00:13:50.342 --> 00:13:53.211
Some buttons were too
small to tap accurately.

00:13:53.647 --> 00:14:00.014
A user with limited hand control could
easily miss a tap on such buttons?

00:14:00.904 --> 00:14:03.853
Or if they were to attempt
to tap them wrongly.

00:14:05.919 --> 00:14:08.086
I documented all these results,

00:14:08.110 --> 00:14:10.973
provided some actionable fixes
and implementation,

00:14:10.997 --> 00:14:14.838
not forgetting the value and the ask.

00:14:15.334 --> 00:14:20.127
Because that's what we're conducting
this accessibility audit for.

00:14:21.019 --> 00:14:23.063
It's for the ask. It's mainly for the ask.

00:14:24.063 --> 00:14:30.929
The value here is the exposure
of Ecobank to a wider set of customers,

00:14:30.953 --> 00:14:34.060
which would boost
the company's revenue or profit,

00:14:34.490 --> 00:14:40.300
and also Ecobank being perceived as an
inclusive customer-centric African bank.

00:14:42.014 --> 00:14:48.940
For what our customers were going to gain
from this accessibility as a value,

00:14:50.533 --> 00:14:56.594
is that they were going to experience
enhanced experience for all customers,

00:14:56.834 --> 00:14:58.731
including persons with disabilities.

00:14:59.938 --> 00:15:02.448
They were going to get better experiences,

00:15:02.472 --> 00:15:08.929
especially persons with disabilities
engaging with Ecobank's mobile app.

00:15:09.697 --> 00:15:18.177
And for the ask, it was mainly
to rant about what accessibility is,

00:15:18.531 --> 00:15:24.143
and then provide them
with the list of issues

00:15:24.167 --> 00:15:29.968
and then also, the
actionable next steps.

00:15:30.275 --> 00:15:33.518
And after the value has been
communicated as well,

00:15:34.223 --> 00:15:38.827
the final step is the ask, which
is to ask for a budget for accessibility.

00:15:40.808 --> 00:15:42.131
I reached out to Deque.

00:15:42.362 --> 00:15:45.110
Deque is a leading
accessibility consultant,

00:15:45.426 --> 00:15:49.860
and they ran us through
several or numerous meetings

00:15:49.884 --> 00:15:54.512
where they shared with not just my team,
but then with the quality assurance team,

00:15:54.536 --> 00:15:57.217
with the developers
and other relevant stakeholders,

00:15:57.636 --> 00:15:59.560
more of their expertise in the field

00:15:59.946 --> 00:16:05.840
and how their various tools, their
various Axe tools could help Ecobank.

00:16:10.269 --> 00:16:12.354
Okay, so what did change?

00:16:15.241 --> 00:16:21.457
While we—I had gone ahead,
conducted the accessibility audit,

00:16:21.481 --> 00:16:23.805
it was now the time to present my findings.

00:16:24.262 --> 00:16:26.575
So I went ahead,
presented my findings,

00:16:26.815 --> 00:16:29.781
and then they did like the idea
of digital accessibility,

00:16:30.120 --> 00:16:33.234
and they hadn't really
paid much attention to it

00:16:33.258 --> 00:16:35.825
or they were delighted.

00:16:36.136 --> 00:16:39.679
They were delighted to have
gotten this feedback

00:16:39.703 --> 00:16:44.333
from the audit
that was gotten or conducted.

00:16:45.984 --> 00:16:50.933
What changed actually was that
accessibility became a compulsory course

00:16:52.051 --> 00:16:54.278
for everyone here at Ecobank to take on,

00:16:54.302 --> 00:16:58.157
to understand truly in
depth what accessibility was.

00:16:58.748 --> 00:17:08.082
And also it was to make—to reach a
wider range of teams in the company

00:17:08.106 --> 00:17:10.154
to understand what accessibility is.

00:17:10.675 --> 00:17:15.768
And then also a budget was drawn
or is yet to be drawn.

00:17:16.183 --> 00:17:21.444
Yes, a budget is yet to be drawn
because as at the time the audit was done

00:17:21.444 --> 00:17:23.177
and then I presented the findings,

00:17:23.201 --> 00:17:26.187
the budget for the year
had already been drawn,

00:17:26.211 --> 00:17:31.705
but then accessibility has been
taken into consideration for 2027.

00:17:33.468 --> 00:17:37.583
Then also another thing that changed was

00:17:37.607 --> 00:17:44.601
we got the most important teams, that's
the developers, our quality assurance team,

00:17:45.016 --> 00:17:48.355
and also other relevant stakeholders

00:17:48.379 --> 00:17:54.310
to truly understand
what digital accessibility is

00:17:54.334 --> 00:17:59.433
and how Ecobank is going to
benefit from digital accessibility

00:17:59.457 --> 00:18:03.635
or making our digital product
accessible to persons with disability.

00:18:03.644 --> 00:18:06.859
And all this was done
and gotten from Deque

00:18:07.705 --> 00:18:09.812
while they were running
us through their sessions.

00:18:10.964 --> 00:18:15.819
So now what has the audit taught me about
making accessibility a business case?

00:18:16.222 --> 00:18:21.514
If you were to ask me again to conduct
an accessibility all over again

00:18:21.979 --> 00:18:24.694
for the past or for the future

00:18:25.153 --> 00:18:29.555
and to make a business case out of it
for the company I work with,

00:18:29.968 --> 00:18:31.252
I would say firstly,

00:18:31.937 --> 00:18:36.347
findings alone do not move the people
you are going to be doing the ask for.

00:18:37.322 --> 00:18:38.581
So what does this mean?

00:18:40.561 --> 00:18:47.539
Instead of just listing out a checklist of,
"We found this,

00:18:48.443 --> 00:18:49.988
these are the issues we found,"

00:18:50.012 --> 00:18:54.379
and then you are just going to
present it to the stakeholders,

00:18:55.949 --> 00:18:59.977
they are not really going
to be moved by that.

00:19:01.905 --> 00:19:03.026
A little bit of a story.

00:19:03.096 --> 00:19:06.797
I remember
when I was in the research phase,

00:19:07.086 --> 00:19:09.630
there was a whole lot
I was learning about accessibility.

00:19:09.654 --> 00:19:11.895
What accessibility is,

00:19:12.834 --> 00:19:18.499
the various laws that were backing
the European countries and America.

00:19:18.782 --> 00:19:21.305
I was learning a whole lot
about digital accessibility,

00:19:21.305 --> 00:19:23.127
and I felt so excited about it.

00:19:23.928 --> 00:19:25.677
Felt so super excited about it.

00:19:26.174 --> 00:19:28.078
And I felt that,

00:19:28.102 --> 00:19:32.587
okay, these people I am going to be making
the business case from,

00:19:32.611 --> 00:19:34.898
they are going to feel
the same way I'm feeling right now

00:19:34.922 --> 00:19:36.636
with all this information I have.

00:19:38.655 --> 00:19:41.130
I was done with the research phase.

00:19:41.156 --> 00:19:43.202
I drafted the report,

00:19:43.621 --> 00:19:45.325
and then I met with my boss

00:19:46.141 --> 00:19:47.950
and I was presenting to him

00:19:47.974 --> 00:19:52.650
just to prep me for the final
meeting day with the stakeholders.

00:19:53.062 --> 00:19:57.508
And after I was done with my preparations,
my presentation,

00:19:57.889 --> 00:20:01.844
he was like, "Okay, I like the fact
that you are learning a lot.

00:20:02.140 --> 00:20:04.975
I like that you are so excited
about what you are learning."

00:20:04.999 --> 00:20:10.462
He even made me present to some people
about what I had learned.

00:20:11.313 --> 00:20:16.341
But then he told me that these findings
alone do not move the stakeholders.

00:20:18.140 --> 00:20:19.926
What he meant here was that

00:20:21.438 --> 00:20:23.986
yes, it's okay to present the findings.

00:20:24.732 --> 00:20:27.572
The screenshots of where the issues are,

00:20:27.808 --> 00:20:29.509
what the issues themselves are.

00:20:30.222 --> 00:20:34.389
But then you should do
that while communicating value,

00:20:35.794 --> 00:20:37.276
while communicating the value

00:20:37.915 --> 00:20:41.837
and then speak in numbers
because these people speak in numbers.

00:20:41.861 --> 00:20:43.069
That's their language.

00:20:44.955 --> 00:20:47.214
So I was like, "Okay,

00:20:47.409 --> 00:20:50.160
I have to go back and then really
think this through."

00:20:50.941 --> 00:20:55.746
If I were to go back
to conduct the whole audit again

00:20:55.770 --> 00:21:01.688
or in my future auditing of
digital accessibility on digital products,

00:21:01.712 --> 00:21:07.951
I would say that when you are making
a business case for this especially,

00:21:08.313 --> 00:21:13.767
you should not just list out the issues

00:21:14.045 --> 00:21:17.873
and then hope that they would understand
what you are talking about.

00:21:18.668 --> 00:21:22.543
But then you should be able
to communicate in their language.

00:21:22.785 --> 00:21:25.175
That is, pick up the numbers.

00:21:26.043 --> 00:21:28.522
How are other companies doing this?

00:21:29.189 --> 00:21:30.675
How are other companies doing this?

00:21:30.699 --> 00:21:34.930
How are the other companies
benefiting from this opportunity?

00:21:35.128 --> 00:21:39.492
How are they benefiting from investing
in digital accessibility?

00:21:40.565 --> 00:21:42.748
How is their return on investment?

00:21:43.996 --> 00:21:49.717
You should be able to bring out
numbers from this to get them on board.

00:21:50.044 --> 00:21:53.713
To move them that, okay,
if other companies are doing this

00:21:53.737 --> 00:21:56.291
and then they are really
reaping a whole lot,

00:21:56.315 --> 00:21:59.703
then we would do the same.

00:22:01.206 --> 00:22:04.096
So while you've communicated the value,

00:22:04.120 --> 00:22:06.590
you've communicated the numbers,

00:22:08.575 --> 00:22:14.069
keep making sure you are using
the language the stakeholders speak.

00:22:14.314 --> 00:22:16.376
So what are some languages
the stakeholders speak?

00:22:16.548 --> 00:22:18.928
As a UX/UI designer,

00:22:20.613 --> 00:22:23.679
one of the most important
flows I have come to discover

00:22:26.579 --> 00:22:29.401
is the onboarding flow
or the onboarding process.

00:22:29.416 --> 00:22:34.675
So why do I say the onboarding process
or flow of a digital product is important?

00:22:35.304 --> 00:22:37.546
It's quite important because—

00:22:38.739 --> 00:22:40.047
It's very important, not quite.

00:22:40.071 --> 00:22:41.925
It's very important because

00:22:43.458 --> 00:22:48.117
this is where your users are
trying to get into your digital product

00:22:48.141 --> 00:22:51.175
to buy your services and
to buy your products from.

00:22:52.969 --> 00:22:57.548
You should try as much as possible
to make that flow, make that screen,

00:22:57.559 --> 00:23:02.665
make that process zero frustrations,
no frustrations at all,

00:23:03.331 --> 00:23:10.011
and remove every friction because
your users, they are making the decision,

00:23:10.035 --> 00:23:15.889
"Okay, I get here, I am entering my
details, and then I click on the button,

00:23:15.913 --> 00:23:18.710
and I go into the app, and then I go
and make the purchases."

00:23:21.282 --> 00:23:24.454
These are key areas stakeholders
pay particular attention to.

00:23:25.257 --> 00:23:29.895
Under these key areas, they pay particular
attention to especially drop-offs—

00:23:29.919 --> 00:23:31.313
keywords like drop-offs.

00:23:31.337 --> 00:23:38.740
So, an example: assume I'm presenting
this business case to you as a stakeholder.

00:23:38.997 --> 00:23:44.745
So I would say that during the audit,
I found out that in the onboarding phase

00:23:45.169 --> 00:23:48.374
or in our onboarding screen
or on our onboarding screen,

00:23:49.796 --> 00:23:56.381
I found out that, these customers,
our customers are able to go through—

00:23:57.473 --> 00:24:04.425
by inputting the details
that is required from them.

00:24:04.833 --> 00:24:08.118
But then
when they get to clicking the button,

00:24:09.798 --> 00:24:15.994
a particular user who uses a screen
reader is not able to move on

00:24:16.018 --> 00:24:21.931
because the screen reader is not able to
spell out what they are tapping on.

00:24:22.193 --> 00:24:25.984
So that is a friction there,
and that causes a drop-off.

00:24:25.984 --> 00:24:31.246
So they will just drop off and then go
find another app to buy from.

00:24:31.561 --> 00:24:34.250
Or a fellow competitor's app to use.

00:24:35.453 --> 00:24:41.432
Pay particular attention to the keywords
stakeholder likes to hear, if I should say,

00:24:41.456 --> 00:24:43.469
for lack of a better word,
like to hear.

00:24:44.840 --> 00:24:47.806
Also, while you are communicating all this,

00:24:47.830 --> 00:24:50.382
make sure you are doing it
in a storytelling form.

00:24:52.061 --> 00:24:56.282
Don't just write a report
and then you are going from,

00:24:56.306 --> 00:25:00.032
"Okay, we mentioned that we
saw that there's this, there's that,

00:25:00.056 --> 00:25:01.747
there's this, there's that."

00:25:01.784 --> 00:25:09.442
You should be able to put the
stakeholders in the shoes of these people.

00:25:09.923 --> 00:25:13.826
How would a person with disability feel

00:25:14.566 --> 00:25:18.006
when they are going
through our various flows

00:25:18.030 --> 00:25:21.625
or screens in our mobile app
or on our mobile app?

00:25:21.922 --> 00:25:24.255
How are they—What are the feelings?

00:25:24.433 --> 00:25:25.701
What are the frictions?

00:25:25.862 --> 00:25:30.862
You should be able to put these
stakeholders in the shoes of these people,

00:25:31.836 --> 00:25:37.006
so they're able to truly feel
what these people are feeling

00:25:37.407 --> 00:25:39.288
or what the customers are feeling,

00:25:39.312 --> 00:25:44.390
and then they get a better sense of, "Okay,
we really need to rectify this issue."

00:25:45.393 --> 00:25:47.699
So as you are going
ahead with the storytelling,

00:25:47.723 --> 00:25:49.890
you are also telling
them about the impact.

00:25:49.890 --> 00:25:53.337
What is in it for Ecobank
as you are spelling it out.

00:25:53.963 --> 00:25:56.044
If you've communicated the value,

00:25:56.391 --> 00:25:59.111
you've communicated the key--
you've used keywords,

00:26:00.935 --> 00:26:08.935
another thing I would recommend a lot
is using real-world examples

00:26:09.295 --> 00:26:11.977
when you are making your business case.

00:26:15.692 --> 00:26:18.067
These relevant stakeholders,
they would want to know

00:26:19.389 --> 00:26:22.493
how truly is this being—

00:26:22.845 --> 00:26:27.429
how truly is this digital accessibility
benefiting other companies?

00:26:28.208 --> 00:26:31.797
I want to know.
Show me: what are other companies doing?

00:26:32.113 --> 00:26:36.529
You need to be able to benchmark
with other companies.

00:26:37.474 --> 00:26:39.199
What are they gaining from it?

00:26:39.199 --> 00:26:42.199
Is it shooting up their profits?
Is it… what?

00:26:43.039 --> 00:26:44.374
You get it, right? Okay.

00:26:47.444 --> 00:26:50.311
There are some example,
there are a whole lot of examples

00:26:50.619 --> 00:26:53.676
of how digital accessibility is—

00:26:54.961 --> 00:26:58.202
companies are benefiting
from digital accessibility.

00:27:00.626 --> 00:27:04.390
Another world example of how
digital accessibility is impacting

00:27:04.414 --> 00:27:07.394
the return on investment of companies,

00:27:08.495 --> 00:27:11.616
is that you would…

00:27:13.988 --> 00:27:16.080
So in the example you are setting,

00:27:16.686 --> 00:27:21.643
again, do not forget to communicate
the value, the numbers.

00:27:22.360 --> 00:27:24.143
and then the examples.

00:27:24.436 --> 00:27:27.803
So an example of the values
you can communicate here.

00:27:28.092 --> 00:27:31.036
Sorry, an example of the numbers
you can communicate here

00:27:31.779 --> 00:27:39.514
is the combined spending power
of people with disabilities

00:27:39.538 --> 00:27:45.128
and their family globally is $13 trillion.

00:27:46.350 --> 00:27:49.047
Yes, you heard that right: $13 trillion.

00:27:49.071 --> 00:27:52.974
If you had to combine the spending
power of people with disabilities

00:27:52.998 --> 00:27:54.592
and their family globally,

00:27:55.438 --> 00:28:00.721
that makes up 13 billion—$13 trillion.

00:28:01.567 --> 00:28:03.796
This isn't just a niche market.

00:28:04.032 --> 00:28:11.132
It's a market leadership,
everyone is already trying to capture

00:28:11.156 --> 00:28:14.304
and probably they just don't know that…

00:28:14.328 --> 00:28:17.575
They just don't know it
in the accessibility sense yet.

00:28:17.924 --> 00:28:20.894
Okay, so another example you could use

00:28:21.398 --> 00:28:27.532
or you could communicate to
your stakeholder is the example of Tesco.

00:28:27.897 --> 00:28:33.918
So Tesco is a British multinational
grocery and general merchandise retailer.

00:28:34.684 --> 00:28:42.683
In 2001, they spent roughly £35,000 on
accessibility improvements to their site.

00:28:43.087 --> 00:28:49.000
And this investment they made
unlocked £30 million in revenue

00:28:49.237 --> 00:28:50.618
just in a single year.

00:28:51.791 --> 00:28:55.253
So you can tell that
this landmark case study

00:28:55.277 --> 00:28:59.596
demonstrated significant, financial impact
of inclusive design

00:28:59.620 --> 00:29:05.045
and on user engagement and conversions.

00:29:06.395 --> 00:29:12.302
And lastly, an example you can use
is the Forrester's research.

00:29:12.501 --> 00:29:16.283
So according to Forrester,
if you indicate…

00:29:16.977 --> 00:29:22.358
Sorry, according to Forrester,
if you invest $1

00:29:23.547 --> 00:29:26.422
or if you spend $1 on UX,

00:29:26.446 --> 00:29:29.484
it returns $100.

00:29:29.932 --> 00:29:33.726
So with accessibility being under UX,

00:29:35.052 --> 00:29:39.128
we can see that if you spend $1
on accessibility,

00:29:39.644 --> 00:29:44.007
that means
you are investing in accessibility,

00:29:44.031 --> 00:29:47.847
and that can yield up
to about 100,000% of returns.

00:29:48.707 --> 00:29:50.941
So these are
a list of examples you can use,

00:29:51.946 --> 00:30:02.903
research from Forrester on the $1 spending
that yields $100 back as return,

00:30:03.291 --> 00:30:08.167
and then how Tesco benefited
from investing in accessibility,

00:30:09.150 --> 00:30:11.998
digital accessibility on their website.

00:30:12.233 --> 00:30:16.027
And then you can think also
about the spending power,

00:30:16.366 --> 00:30:19.756
the combined spending power of persons
with disability and their family,

00:30:19.780 --> 00:30:22.312
which makes up $13 trillion.

00:30:24.464 --> 00:30:30.336
Okay. So to round up all my learnings
and my experiences,

00:30:30.740 --> 00:30:34.522
there are some tips that I would leave
for you or with you

00:30:34.546 --> 00:30:36.511
when you are making a business case.

00:30:36.751 --> 00:30:41.334
And yes, I would highly recommend that
even if you are not thinking about it,

00:30:41.334 --> 00:30:45.746
you should start thinking about
preparing yourself

00:30:45.770 --> 00:30:48.832
to make a business case
for digital accessibility,

00:30:48.856 --> 00:30:50.761
because I believe every company,

00:30:51.931 --> 00:30:52.980
not just companies,

00:30:53.004 --> 00:30:57.630
every company, every business should
be able to think digital accessibility

00:30:57.654 --> 00:31:05.181
because, you are really missing out
on the 16% of the population,

00:31:06.314 --> 00:31:09.193
that could be your new market.

00:31:10.576 --> 00:31:13.261
Which in turn boosts your revenue.

00:31:14.739 --> 00:31:19.638
So one tip I could leave you with

00:31:19.662 --> 00:31:23.275
is you need to be able
to lead with something tangible.

00:31:26.139 --> 00:31:28.057
When you are starting the audit,

00:31:28.342 --> 00:31:31.780
you need to first of all prepare
your stakeholders and your team.

00:31:33.298 --> 00:31:35.033
Have them prepared.

00:31:35.057 --> 00:31:38.394
Don't just go ahead,
conduct the thing, the audit,

00:31:38.418 --> 00:31:41.476
and then just come slam it in their face

00:31:41.974 --> 00:31:43.947
and then expect them to give you the ask.

00:31:43.971 --> 00:31:49.750
I mean, it, it may work, but if you really
want it to be like a fast achievement,

00:31:50.085 --> 00:31:54.895
for a faster cheat code, I
would say that it should involve them.

00:31:55.745 --> 00:32:03.610
And then you don't necessarily have to
make an ask for conducting the audit

00:32:03.634 --> 00:32:05.117
in order to present it to them.

00:32:05.471 --> 00:32:07.770
So like I mentioned earlier,

00:32:08.456 --> 00:32:10.112
when I was conducting the audit,

00:32:10.136 --> 00:32:14.752
I used Accessibility Scanner
as an automated tool.

00:32:15.584 --> 00:32:18.099
There are several other
automated tools you could use.

00:32:18.099 --> 00:32:20.922
Accessibility Scanner
for your Android apps,

00:32:21.981 --> 00:32:23.135
you can also use WAVE.

00:32:23.507 --> 00:32:27.808
WAVE is for—is a checklist.

00:32:27.832 --> 00:32:31.344
It's a checking tool
for websites especially.

00:32:32.320 --> 00:32:34.661
So there are a lot
of automated tools you could use.

00:32:34.661 --> 00:32:39.450
So I would say that you should
start with prepping them

00:32:39.717 --> 00:32:44.292
and then in your audit
phase or process,

00:32:45.629 --> 00:32:48.372
Get free tools.

00:32:48.396 --> 00:32:51.973
They would… Even though
these free tools do not,

00:32:53.431 --> 00:32:58.290
they do not capture 100 of all the issues
because yes, you need automated tools,

00:32:58.545 --> 00:33:00.579
but then you would also need to test with—

00:33:01.568 --> 00:33:05.027
you would also need to test manually
with persons with disabilites.

00:33:05.300 --> 00:33:08.638
So you can't just depend
on 100% on automated tools.

00:33:09.079 --> 00:33:14.033
But then as the start,
just to get your stakeholders in there

00:33:15.688 --> 00:33:19.913
in order to get them to
bring out the budgets.

00:33:20.851 --> 00:33:24.131
I would say that you get started
with free automated tools,

00:33:25.449 --> 00:33:28.909
and these free automated tools are enough
for you to make your business case.

00:33:34.189 --> 00:33:40.098
I've already mentioned some automated
tools like the Accessibility Scanner.

00:33:40.369 --> 00:33:45.043
So how that works is that after you have
downloaded it and you've activated it,

00:33:45.067 --> 00:33:47.120
there's a floating blue button

00:33:47.144 --> 00:33:50.846
where it takes screenshots or
screen records of multiple pages.

00:33:50.847 --> 00:33:54.409
So a screenshot if it's a single page,

00:33:54.433 --> 00:33:56.171
and you can take a screen record

00:33:56.171 --> 00:34:01.932
and it's able to capture
issues on multiple screens.

00:34:02.699 --> 00:34:07.021
So yeah,
after you've taken the screen records

00:34:07.045 --> 00:34:09.221
or you've taken the snapshots,

00:34:09.997 --> 00:34:13.963
it's also able to give you
a whole documentation

00:34:14.679 --> 00:34:24.129
that speaks in depth
into the issue at hand.

00:34:24.287 --> 00:34:26.728
So it's gonna list
out the issues on the screen,

00:34:26.752 --> 00:34:28.217
and then when you tap on them,

00:34:28.241 --> 00:34:31.759
it's going to tell you
just a summary of it,

00:34:31.783 --> 00:34:36.074
and then it's going to give you a whole
documentation that explains into it,

00:34:36.903 --> 00:34:37.912
what the issues are.

00:34:38.966 --> 00:34:41.570
And then another tip I would say
is to remember to speak

00:34:41.594 --> 00:34:43.138
in the language of the stakeholder.

00:34:44.055 --> 00:34:45.925
Remember to use stakeholder languages.

00:34:47.039 --> 00:34:52.436
Example, the keywords such as conversions,
return on investment, retentions

00:34:53.047 --> 00:34:55.799
not just going about accessibility
as a keyword.

00:34:57.229 --> 00:35:02.649
And also it's crucial to benchmark how
accessibility is greatly impacting other companies.

00:35:02.666 --> 00:35:05.131
So you have to do your research
on these areas,

00:35:05.433 --> 00:35:09.136
how it's benefiting other companies,

00:35:09.160 --> 00:35:11.949
and then you communicate that.

00:35:12.820 --> 00:35:14.685
and not forgetting the numbers.

00:35:15.968 --> 00:35:19.858
And then also lastly, the last tip I would
say is you do not have to do it alone.

00:35:20.688 --> 00:35:25.360
I highly believe that accessibility
has to be… Everyone has to be involved.

00:35:25.598 --> 00:35:27.527
So get the relevant team on board,

00:35:27.551 --> 00:35:32.367
your developers, your quality assurance
team, project managers, product managers.

00:35:32.391 --> 00:35:36.984
Get everyone on board,
and it'll be a great start.

00:35:42.469 --> 00:35:48.075
Okay. So, I left out here some links

00:35:48.099 --> 00:35:52.717
to having access to the tools
I have recommended.

00:35:52.982 --> 00:35:56.475
That's Axe, and then also there's
a video here that takes you through,

00:35:56.475 --> 00:35:59.005
how to use Accessibility Scanner.

00:35:59.315 --> 00:36:02.299
And also there are some
articles here you can read,

00:36:02.630 --> 00:36:05.462
further read on how to make
a business case for digital accessibility.

00:36:06.174 --> 00:36:08.788
So, thank you very much. I am Priscilla.

00:36:08.788 --> 00:36:12.431
You can find me on LinkedIn
as Priscilla Afia Opoku.

00:36:12.674 --> 00:36:17.455
And then also you can hit me up on email,
and then we can discuss further.

00:36:18.221 --> 00:36:21.489
Any questions you have,
you can hit me up on email,

00:36:21.513 --> 00:36:24.940
you can hit me up on LinkedIn,
and thank you very much.

00:36:29.444 --> 00:36:30.589
<v Gary> Thank you, Priscilla.

00:36:30.589 --> 00:36:31.826
That was wonderful.

00:36:32.479 --> 00:36:36.566
Yeah, that was a really great insight

00:36:36.590 --> 00:36:41.098
into how to do a
website accessibility audit

00:36:41.122 --> 00:36:44.284
and really good to see it from inside,

00:36:44.895 --> 00:36:49.356
with an organization that's
doing that for the first time.

00:36:49.422 --> 00:36:52.131
Yeah. So thank you very
much for that. That's great.

00:36:54.269 --> 00:36:57.041
I'm here in Nottingham in the UK

00:36:57.065 --> 00:36:59.548
and Robin Hood sends his apologies.

00:36:59.551 --> 00:37:00.690
He's on another call.

00:37:00.690 --> 00:37:01.761
Sorry about that.

00:37:03.403 --> 00:37:05.756
Yeah, but we've got some questions
for you, Priscilla.

00:37:08.952 --> 00:37:10.853
The first question I have here is,

00:37:12.252 --> 00:37:16.911
Yeah, you mentioned talking to
the chief digital officer in Ecobank.

00:37:18.753 --> 00:37:23.674
What approach did you use to keep
those high-level stakeholders engaged

00:37:24.163 --> 00:37:26.872
during your pre-audit preparation?

00:37:26.879 --> 00:37:34.160
Because obviously you can overwhelm
people with the kind of technical WCAG,

00:37:34.184 --> 00:37:37.770
you know, all the
technical details of accessibility,

00:37:37.794 --> 00:37:40.876
but how did you… You talked
a little bit about storytelling,

00:37:40.900 --> 00:37:43.418
but is there anything
more you can say about that?

00:37:44.717 --> 00:37:45.728
<v Priscilla> Yes.

00:37:48.855 --> 00:37:51.688
Just like you mentioned,
I used a storytelling approach,

00:37:51.712 --> 00:37:57.797
and then also I didn't bore them with
the technical language, if I should say.

00:37:57.821 --> 00:38:00.418
'cause it's not really
a language they speak.

00:38:01.060 --> 00:38:04.827
So I spoke in a language they understand,

00:38:05.627 --> 00:38:09.920
breaking down into normal English language,
not using technical words.

00:38:10.515 --> 00:38:14.628
And then also yes, that's basically it.

00:38:15.105 --> 00:38:19.428
Just bringing it down to, like,
not using technical words

00:38:19.698 --> 00:38:21.826
and normal everyday English language,

00:38:22.396 --> 00:38:25.659
and then saying that
in a storytelling form.

00:38:26.087 --> 00:38:29.959
And then also not forgetting
to communicate the values.

00:38:30.357 --> 00:38:33.967
And then also there are keywords

00:38:33.991 --> 00:38:37.235
'cause they like to hear those keywords,
I think. Yeah.

00:38:38.942 --> 00:38:41.279
<v Gary> Yeah, you're talking about
everyday language there.

00:38:41.303 --> 00:38:42.351
That's really important.

00:38:43.661 --> 00:38:48.218
Yeah, I just wanna say that I was amazed
by those figures you were talking about

00:38:48.242 --> 00:38:51.883
in terms of—'cause
Tesco is my local supermarket,

00:38:51.964 --> 00:38:55.061
you know, 10 minutes’ walk down the road,
and I'm like, "Wow,

00:38:55.557 --> 00:38:58.581
that is an amazing
return on investment there."

00:39:01.724 --> 00:39:04.891
I just got another question,

00:39:06.022 --> 00:39:09.929
someone's asking, "My clients
are family-owned local businesses,

00:39:09.953 --> 00:39:14.481
and they don't think they'll reach
beyond their small town or location.

00:39:15.000 --> 00:39:21.561
I'm just wondering if you have any
examples you could use to persuade them

00:39:22.296 --> 00:39:25.594
that accessibility is worth doing."

00:39:25.605 --> 00:39:30.716
'Cause you were talking about how
many people are disabled.

00:39:30.732 --> 00:39:34.488
For example, I think I did the math;
there's 7% of Ghanaians

00:39:34.488 --> 00:39:36.500
that have a disability.

00:39:38.486 --> 00:39:42.884
So yeah,
is that something that you could use

00:39:42.908 --> 00:39:47.482
to address that with a small business?

00:39:50.167 --> 00:39:51.782
<v Priscilla> If I get your question right,

00:39:51.806 --> 00:39:54.164
Can you kindly ask that again?

00:39:55.364 --> 00:39:56.428
<v Gary> Yeah, sorry, I was just—

00:39:56.452 --> 00:40:00.142
someone was saying that my clients
are family-owned small businesses,

00:40:00.166 --> 00:40:01.341
local businesses,

00:40:01.365 --> 00:40:05.313
so they don't think they've got
a reach beyond the small town.

00:40:07.757 --> 00:40:09.080
So yeah, I guess they're asking,

00:40:09.080 --> 00:40:14.132
"How can they be persuaded that
accessibility is worth doing for them?"

00:40:15.796 --> 00:40:23.623
<v Priscilla> Okay. So if
they do have a digital product,

00:40:24.822 --> 00:40:29.471
they use for their business,

00:40:29.840 --> 00:40:32.650
then I'd say they should
still do accessibility

00:40:32.674 --> 00:40:37.647
because I'm pretty sure in the long run,
it could help bring more customers.

00:40:39.341 --> 00:40:43.777
So telling a friend to tell a friend,
promoting your business.

00:40:43.809 --> 00:40:49.169
You may never know who is getting involved
or being your customer in your business.

00:40:49.987 --> 00:40:54.557
So whether small or big,
you should still do digital accessibility.

00:40:57.243 --> 00:40:58.345
<v Gary> Yeah. Thank you. Yeah.

00:41:02.116 --> 00:41:11.831
Obviously, 7% of potential customers is a
big number for a small business, isn't it?

00:41:11.831 --> 00:41:15.461
It's like potentially
getting a real big uplift there.

00:41:16.334 --> 00:41:18.470
I was just really impressed by Ecobank

00:41:18.494 --> 00:41:24.942
because if you don't yet have those laws
in Ghana about accessibility,

00:41:24.966 --> 00:41:27.516
but they're still saying, "Yes,
we want to do this anyway."

00:41:28.000 --> 00:41:30.331
So they're going to be ahead of the curve

00:41:30.975 --> 00:41:33.364
when those laws will come in one day

00:41:33.947 --> 00:41:39.197
and any company
that's already done all that work,

00:41:41.854 --> 00:41:42.936
that's amazing.

00:41:45.219 --> 00:41:47.110
And we've just got another question here.

00:41:47.110 --> 00:41:50.685
I was just wondering if you could come
a little bit closer to your microphone

00:41:50.709 --> 00:41:53.437
so we can hear you a bit better,
if that's okay.

00:41:53.875 --> 00:41:56.249
But one question is:

00:41:56.273 --> 00:42:01.721
how did you balance the automated findings
from the Google Accessibility Scanner

00:42:02.586 --> 00:42:09.882
with the need to present the stories,
the human stories to your board?

00:42:11.072 --> 00:42:13.100
How did you manage the balance?

00:42:14.919 --> 00:42:16.875
<v Priscilla> Okay. So like I mentioned earlier,

00:42:18.278 --> 00:42:21.043
I started with the research findings.

00:42:23.223 --> 00:42:28.815
My research findings initially started with
understanding what accessibility truly was.

00:42:30.061 --> 00:42:36.223
So I was looking at what it is and how
it's… the benefits it comes with.

00:42:36.578 --> 00:42:42.100
I was really watching videos of how
the various people with disabilities

00:42:42.344 --> 00:42:44.378
use their digital products.

00:42:44.693 --> 00:42:47.101
So how a blind person uses their phone,

00:42:47.125 --> 00:42:50.972
how they are able to
maneuver through a digital product

00:42:51.864 --> 00:42:54.203
to buy something or to use a service.

00:42:54.961 --> 00:42:56.385
So I started with that

00:42:56.949 --> 00:43:01.432
and then I presented on that phase

00:43:02.148 --> 00:43:05.862
And then after I did that,
that was when I went searching

00:43:05.886 --> 00:43:07.632
and then the numbers
and everything.

00:43:08.168 --> 00:43:12.190
So I did that as a strategy
to get them on board,

00:43:12.214 --> 00:43:14.147
to get them hooked with the stories,

00:43:14.171 --> 00:43:17.229
and then I used the numbers later on.

00:43:19.004 --> 00:43:20.671
So that was the balance.

00:43:22.212 --> 00:43:26.470
<v Gary> Absolutely, yeah. So you're building
a story about, oh, this person…

00:43:26.470 --> 00:43:29.299
And we were talking about accessibility,

00:43:31.234 --> 00:43:38.426
in terms of actually seeing
personas of, like, this is a real person

00:43:38.450 --> 00:43:40.141
and this is how they use the internet,

00:43:41.417 --> 00:43:43.992
So you were putting
that in as well as the figures.

00:43:47.517 --> 00:43:50.741
Yeah, you talked about
getting buy-in from the customer

00:43:50.765 --> 00:43:52.816
and cross-functional readiness.

00:43:54.221 --> 00:44:02.043
Again, amazing that the Ecobank, they have a
compulsory accessibility course for everyone.

00:44:02.105 --> 00:44:04.020
That's amazing.

00:44:06.079 --> 00:44:11.472
Is there anything else that helps
with getting people on board, do you think

00:44:12.538 --> 00:44:14.254
 in terms of that buy-in?

00:44:17.621 --> 00:44:21.067
<v Priscilla> Yes. So I think first of all,
it's the knowledge,

00:44:21.605 --> 00:44:25.013
spreading the knowledge,
on digital accessibility.

00:44:25.518 --> 00:44:29.534
So after we had met with the stakeholders,

00:44:29.858 --> 00:44:32.797
my boss gave us a task.

00:44:33.058 --> 00:44:37.831
So, in my place of work, that's Ecobank,

00:44:38.204 --> 00:44:41.877
we do have this community on Teams,

00:44:42.263 --> 00:44:47.376
where everybody on board
from around the various branches

00:44:47.400 --> 00:44:48.818
in the different countries,

00:44:49.158 --> 00:44:50.440
we are all on that community.

00:44:50.810 --> 00:44:57.253
So my boss tasked us to be posting
about UX, about accessibility,

00:44:57.277 --> 00:45:01.809
and of course I was sharing
videos on Be My Eyes,

00:45:01.833 --> 00:45:03.620
how Be My Eyes is being used.

00:45:03.749 --> 00:45:07.616
I was sharing videos
or articles on digital accessibility.

00:45:07.953 --> 00:45:12.431
And then we had people like
viewing this post, commenting about it.

00:45:12.906 --> 00:45:16.205
And then before we knew it,
it was like a mandatory course,

00:45:16.801 --> 00:45:18.490
for everyone to be on board.

00:45:18.490 --> 00:45:23.582
And then also for the developers

00:45:23.606 --> 00:45:31.270
and then for the technical guys
in our my place of work, that's Ecobank.

00:45:33.559 --> 00:45:36.867
My boss made me understand that

00:45:37.720 --> 00:45:41.134
or through my research, I found out that

00:45:42.637 --> 00:45:45.885
You can't do the business case alone.

00:45:46.712 --> 00:45:48.924
You need to have everybody else on board.

00:45:48.924 --> 00:45:52.202
Because as a designer,
I would design the website,

00:45:52.226 --> 00:45:53.494
I would design the mobile app,

00:45:54.375 --> 00:45:56.329
but then I hand off to the developer.

00:45:56.980 --> 00:46:01.556
So if the developer has no idea about
accessibility or digital accessibility,

00:46:01.580 --> 00:46:05.272
he's just gonna go about his normal day
of coding and not even thinking about it.

00:46:06.841 --> 00:46:10.855
And then he's just gonna
publish what he has to publish,

00:46:10.879 --> 00:46:12.210
and then they move on.

00:46:12.237 --> 00:46:15.063
But then when you bring
these people on board,

00:46:15.319 --> 00:46:17.847
you get them to understand
little by little,

00:46:18.197 --> 00:46:21.951
and then they get to appreciate it,
and then they include it.

00:46:22.116 --> 00:46:27.287
So I remember there was this time
I went to see one of my bosses

00:46:28.540 --> 00:46:34.839
about the way forward with digital
accessibility when it comes to Ecobank.

00:46:34.881 --> 00:46:37.811
And then he was like, "Oh, for 2026,

00:46:38.643 --> 00:46:41.636
he's already had conversations
with the developers

00:46:42.101 --> 00:46:45.096
 to include
digital accessibility in their code.

00:46:45.470 --> 00:46:49.737
So he's like since the research,
they're making inclusiveness in their code.

00:46:50.342 --> 00:46:52.562
But then for 2027,

00:46:52.586 --> 00:46:58.741
he's going to make sure that a proper
budget is drafted for digital accessibility

00:47:01.229 --> 00:47:02.229
<v Gary> Excellent. Yeah.

00:47:02.229 --> 00:47:07.229
So yeah, it's a good point that developers
need to be on board as well,

00:47:07.928 --> 00:47:10.796
because they've already got
so many other things to think about,

00:47:10.820 --> 00:47:12.210
so they're like, "Oh, yeah."

00:47:12.210 --> 00:47:15.296
This is another thing that you
need that buy-in, don't you?

00:47:16.399 --> 00:47:17.444
Excellent.

00:47:17.478 --> 00:47:20.442
Well, just a final question.

00:47:20.446 --> 00:47:21.590
Do you think that it's—

00:47:21.877 --> 00:47:22.942
just in one minute—

00:47:22.966 --> 00:47:25.792
do you think
it's already made a difference?

00:47:25.792 --> 00:47:32.546
Have you seen more people
becoming Ecobank customers with this,

00:47:32.570 --> 00:47:33.894
or is it too early to say?

00:47:35.119 --> 00:47:36.212
<v Priscilla> I think it's too early.

00:47:37.912 --> 00:47:41.105
<v Gary> Yeah? No, well,
it's amazing work you've done.

00:47:41.186 --> 00:47:46.421
And yeah, really impressive that
Ecobank have got on board with that.

00:47:46.445 --> 00:47:47.914
 So thank you very much, Priscilla.

00:47:47.914 --> 00:47:50.319
Yeah, really appreciate
your talk this morning

00:47:51.736 --> 00:47:54.654
or this afternoon, or this evening,
wherever you are in the world.

00:47:54.654 --> 00:47:59.770
So thank you for attending
this session with Priscilla Afia Opoku.

00:47:59.799 --> 00:48:03.907
You can continue the conversation in Slack
or on social media

00:48:03.931 --> 00:48:12.220
using #WPA11yDay and #WPAD2026.